==English==

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|re|new}}, after {{etyl|la}} {{term|renovare}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ɹɪˈnjuː/}}
* {{audio|en-us-renew.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|uː}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{transitive}} To make (something) new again; to [[restore]] to [[freshness]] or original condition. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: ''In such a night Medea gathered the enchanted herbs That did '''renew''' old son.''  -Shak.
# {{transitive}} To [[replace]] (something which has broken etc.); to [[replenish]] (something which has been exhausted), to keep up a required supply of. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{theology}} To make new spiritually; to [[regenerate]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: ''Be ye transformed by the '''renewing''' of your mind.'' Rom. xii. 2.
# {{rft-sense}} {{obsolete|intransitive}} To become new, or as new; to [[revive]]. {{defdate|15th-18th c.}}
#* '''1621''', Robert Burton, ''The Anatomy of Melancholy'', II.2.6.ii:
#*: to such as are in fear they strike a great impression, '''renew''' many times, and recall such chimeras and terrible fictions into their minds.
#* '''2010''' September, Michael Allen, "St. Louis Preservation Fund", [[w:St. Louis Magazine|''St. Louis'' magazine]], ISSN 1090-5723, volume 16, issue 9, page 74:
#*: '''Renewing''' neighborhoods dealing with vacant buildings badly need options other than demolition or dangerous vacant spaces.
# {{transitive}} To begin again; to [[recommence]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: ''The last great age . . . '''renews''' its finished course.''  -Dryden.
# {{rare}} To [[repeat]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: ''The birds-their notes '''renew'''.''  -Milton.

